# macOS one-time provisioning and durable access

Date: 2026-09-08. Branch: `macos-provisioning`.

## Objective and boundaries

An administrator installs and approves PLANK once, then ordinary authorized
desktop users and OS updates do not unexpectedly strand the headless Mac.
This is a goal to qualify, not a claim that macOS grants all consent machine-wide.
Keep ScreenCaptureKit, VideoToolbox, existing audio ownership, graphical-scope
checks and remote account authorization unchanged. No privacy-database edits,
SIP changes, automatic consent clicks, automatic login, reboot or root desktop
capture fallback. The reference PCoIP Mac remains read-only.

## Evidence before implementation

- Current development installation provisions one explicitly named desktop
  account. Other users have neither the desktop LaunchAgent nor a private
  identity prepared. Missing startup must not be diagnosed as denied consent.
- Current app uses `la.instinctual.PLANK.Host` with Apple Development signing.
  An upgrade must preserve the designated requirement and Team ID, not pin the
  changing binary hash. An intentional Developer ID transition is a separate
  approval/qualification step. No notarized-distribution claim yet.
- Installed PCoIP 26.05.3 has a root recovery service. Read-only binary inspection
  found OS-update checks, missing login/lock-screen plugin detection, auth-plugin
  reinstallation and a wait-for-reboot state. This supports repair of login
  integration, not proof that it grants fresh TCC consent. HP's
  [26.05.1 notes](https://anyware.hp.com/components/graphics-agent-for-macos/26.05.1/release-notes)
  describe automatic OS-update recovery without documenting its full mechanism.
- Apple's current [PPPC schema](https://github.com/apple/device-management/blob/release/mdm/profiles/com.apple.TCC.configuration-profile-policy.yaml)
  disallows granting ScreenCapture through that payload, and says Accessibility
  grants through it are removed in macOS 27. PostEvent is a separate policy.
  Do not claim generic MDM enrollment solves every permission. The development
  Mac currently has no MDM enrollment.
- [Persistent Content Capture](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.developer.persistent-content-capture)
  requires Apple approval. Requesting this entitlement needs the account owner's
  participation. Its documentation does not establish blanket initial consent,
  cross-user approval, or audio/input rights. Do not add an unapproved entitlement.

## Phase 1 — measure consent without granting it

Add `--check-permissions` to the actual signed Host app. Emit version, UID,
valid graphical context, ScreenCapture preflight, PostEvent preflight and
Accessibility status. Readiness is screen/input only; audio-tap permission is
explicitly `not-checked`. This opens no listener, captures no content, posts no
input, creates no display/tap and makes no permission request. It is a point-in-
time diagnostic, never a reusable authorization token. Exit 0 means all measured
screen/input conditions pass; 3 means one or more do not; 2 means diagnostic error.

Use a temporary Aqua launchd job for the installed signed executable in the
actual target user's GUI domain. Plain SSH, `sudo -u`, or the root Background
domain cannot establish another user's graphical readiness. Do not replace
the app with a differently identified probe or infer consent from System
Settings appearances. Preserve the working app until candidate signature and
upgrade checks pass. A same-signature installation still needs a real check.

Qualification matrix:

1. Existing approved desktop: record preflights and confirm normal access.
2. New standard account with no manually granted PLANK permissions: record the
   same preflights before opening the permission-request UI.
3. If denied, record exactly which permissions require approval; stop and ask
   the operator. Do not invoke prompt APIs from background jobs.
4. If approved, test real capture/input and same-user desktop audio separately.
   Successful Boolean preflights are not proof of event delivery or audio.
5. Logout/LoginWindow/new desktop/return to original account, then an
   operator-controlled reboot. Never reuse a prior user's stream or TLS key.
6. Same-identity app update; later, operator-scheduled OS update and final macOS
   27 requalification. No automatic OS update or reboot in this task.

## Phase 2 — system-wide product provisioning

After the matrix establishes the permission behavior, replace the single-user
startup arrangement with one root-owned Aqua LaunchAgent in
`/Library/LaunchAgents`, alongside the existing LoginWindow agent. All desktop
workers still run as their actual user; they do not inherit root capture/input.
Use one root-owned public machine configuration for listener address/port/name/
UUID. Generate each user's independent private TLS identity under that user's
authority on first graphical startup; never copy the LoginWindow private key.
Preserve administrator settings and identities on upgrade. No scanning every
home directory, per-login privileged writes into homes, or extra watcher.

Qualify different users, inactive/fast-switched sessions, locked accounts,
ownership handoff, denied cross-account access, user-controlled path/symlink
failures and shared configuration tampering before enabling all-user startup.
Changing the LaunchAgent does not grant macOS privacy consent.

## Phase 3 — durable deployment

Evaluate approved Persistent Content Capture and optional managed PostEvent
policy against the exact macOS 27 APIs we use. Keep any profile opt-in and
derive code requirements from the signed product, not guessed bundle IDs.
Provisioning UI must distinguish service health, permission denial and actual
capture/input/audio readiness. Recheck locally after account/OS transitions;
repair only PLANK-owned service registration/configuration when safe. Do not
reset consent or blindly restart the ownership coordinator while an old
graphical agent may retain resources. Maintain a separately qualified recovery
path until unattended new-user/update tests pass.

## Initial implementation gates

- All 16 screen/input readiness combinations, including missing graphical
  scope, fail closed except the fully permitted state.
- Upgrade identity gate runs before state writes or service shutdown. Reject
  ad-hoc signing, changed team/requirement, invalid signature and app symlinks.
- Compile using SDK/deployment 27 and warnings-as-errors. Preserve whole signed
  app bundle when checking it. No change to shared Linux or transport code.
- Record actual machine tests and the next operator action in HANDOFF; never
  advertise a preflight or unit test as full multi-user acceptance.

## Phase 1 checkpoint

Host1.0.72-macos-provisioning is installed on the dedicated Mac. The original
approved desktop passes all measured screen/input checks after a same-identity
upgrade, without a consent prompt. Plain SSH under that same UID correctly does
not qualify graphical readiness. The operator subsequently logged into the
temporary standard account; its actual Aqua check also passes all measured
screen/input preflights without invoking prompt APIs. The frozen last LoginWindow
frame was caused by that account lacking a desktop worker, not denied preflights.
A narrowly scoped qualification fixture now provisions that account's own
worker/private keys, and native video delivery has started. Operator acceptance
of actual video/input/audio and OS-update durability remain open. System-wide
startup/public configuration is still the next permanent implementation step.
